Yes, Muslims believe in Jesus as a significant prophet of God. Belief in the prophets is one of the pillars of faith in Islam [[qa:icadb:36087]].

{{quran:2:136}}

The Quran teaches that Muslims believe in what was revealed to Moses and Jesus, as well as all other prophets from their Lord [[quran:2:136, quran:3:84]]. It is emphasized that there should be no distinction made between any of these prophets [[quran:2:136, quran:3:84]].

{{quran:3:52}}

Jesus is considered one of the greatest messengers and is among the "Arch-Prophets" (Ulul 'Azm) [[qa:icadb:36090, qa:icadb:36087]]. Muslims believe that Jesus was a servant of God and His messenger, not the son of God [[qa:icadb:36087]]. He is described as being honored and close to God in both this world and the hereafter [[qa:icadb:36090]].

{{quran:5:111}}

Faith in Jesus requires Muslims to believe that he was a messenger of God, which necessitates honoring and loving him while avoiding any exaggeration or taking him as a deity [[qa:icadb:36087]]. It is not permissible in Islam to hate any prophet; doing so would result in leaving the fold of Islam [[qa:icadb:36087]].

{{note:refer}}

حرم الإسلام أكل لحم الخنزير كونه من الخبائث التي قد تضر بصحة الإنسان وعافيته [[qa:icadb:36234]]. ويهدف هذا التحريم إلى تحقيق مقصد الشريعة في حفظ النفس، وهي من الضروريات الخمس [[qa:bayyinat:200]].

